WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.660
IELTS 16, published by Cambridge University Press and Cambridge Assessment English 2021.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.740
This recording is copyright.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.520
Test 1.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.660
This is the IELTS listening test.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:21.580
You will hear a number of different recordings and you will have to answer questions on what

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:22.580
you hear.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:28.360
There will be time for you to read the instructions and questions and you will have a chance to

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.559
check your work.

00:00:30.559 --> 00:00:34.720
All the recordings will be played once only.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.680
The test is in four parts.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.799
At the end of the test, you will be given 10 minutes to transfer your answers to the

00:00:42.799 --> 00:00:45.200
answer sheet.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:52.240
Now turn to part 1.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.320
Part 1.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:01:00.240
You will hear a man phoning to find out about some children's engineering workshops.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:19.760
First, you have some time to look at questions 1 to 3.

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:26.760
Now listen carefully and answer questions 1 to 3.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.160
What sorts of activities do they do?

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:54.160
All sorts.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:00.360
For example, they work together to design a special cover that goes round an egg so

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:05.800
that when it's inside, they can drop it from a height and it doesn't break.

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:09.960
Well, sometimes it does break but that's part of the fun.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:10.960
Right.

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.279
And Lydia loves building things.

00:02:14.279 --> 00:02:17.080
Is there any opportunity for her to do that?

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:22.160
Well, they have a competition to see who can make the highest tower.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:24.600
You'd be amazed how high they can go.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:25.600
Right.

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:29.600
But they're learning all the time as well as having fun.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.919
For example, one thing they do is to design and build a car that's attached to a balloon

00:02:35.919 --> 00:02:41.800
and the force of the air in that actually powers the car and makes it move along.

00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:43.960
They go really fast too.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:44.960
OK.

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.960
Well, all this sounds perfect.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:56.600
Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions 4

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:57.240
to 10.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:34.559
Now listen and answer questions 4 to 10.

00:03:34.559 --> 00:03:41.800
Now Carly, that's my older daughter, has just had her 7th birthday, so presumably she'd

00:03:41.800 --> 00:03:43.520
be in a different group.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:44.520
Yes.

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:47.360
She'd be in the Junior Engineers.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.240
That's for children from 6 to 8.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.160
And do they do the same sorts of activities?

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:56.639
Some are the same but a bit more advanced.

00:03:56.639 --> 00:04:03.600
So they work out how to build model vehicles, things like cars and trucks, but also how

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:09.720
to construct animals using the same sorts of material and technique.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:13.559
And then they learn how they can programme them and make them move.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:15.600
So they learn a bit of coding?

00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:16.600
They do.

00:04:16.600 --> 00:04:18.600
They pick it up really quickly.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.799
We're there to help if they need it, but they learn from one another too.

00:04:23.799 --> 00:04:24.799
Right.

00:04:24.799 --> 00:04:27.559
And do they have competitions too?

00:04:27.559 --> 00:04:28.559
Yes.

00:04:28.559 --> 00:04:36.040
With the Junior Engineers, it's to use recycled materials like card and wood to build a bridge.

00:04:36.040 --> 00:04:38.559
And the longest one gets a prize.

00:04:38.559 --> 00:04:39.559
That sounds fun.

00:04:39.559 --> 00:04:42.279
I wouldn't mind doing that myself.

00:04:42.279 --> 00:04:48.559
And they have something a bit different, which is to think up an idea for a five-minute movie

00:04:48.559 --> 00:04:53.359
and then film it using special animation software.

00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:55.760
You'd be amazed what they come up with.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.359
And of course that's something they can put on their phone and take home to show all their

00:05:00.359 --> 00:05:01.359
friends.

00:05:01.359 --> 00:05:02.359
Exactly.

00:05:02.359 --> 00:05:09.480
And then they also build a robot in the shape of a human and they decorate it and programme

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.040
it so that it can move its arms and legs.

00:05:12.799 --> 00:05:13.799
Perfect.

00:05:13.799 --> 00:05:17.279
So is it the same price as the Tiny Engineers?

00:05:17.279 --> 00:05:21.279
It's just a bit more, £50 for the five weeks.

00:05:21.279 --> 00:05:23.640
And are the classes on a Monday too?

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:28.920
They used to be, but we found it didn't give our staff enough time to clear up after the

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:33.519
first workshop, so we moved them to Wednesdays.

00:05:33.519 --> 00:05:37.720
The classes are held in the morning from 10 to 11.

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:38.799
OK.

00:05:38.799 --> 00:05:41.160
That's better for me actually.

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:42.839
And what about the location?

00:05:42.839 --> 00:05:45.399
Where exactly are the workshops held?

00:05:45.399 --> 00:05:47.760
They're in building 10A.

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:53.440
There's a big sign on the door, you can't miss it, and that's in Fradston Industrial Estate.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:54.440
Sorry?

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:55.440
Fradston.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:05.440
That's F-R-A-D-S-T-O-N-E.

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:08.040
And that's in Grasford, isn't it?

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.239
Yes, up past the station.

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:12.920
And will I have any parking problems there?

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:15.920
No, there's always plenty available.

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:19.119
So would you like to enrol Lydia and Carly now?

00:06:19.119 --> 00:06:20.119
OK.

00:06:20.119 --> 00:06:26.119
So can I have your full name and…

00:06:26.119 --> 00:06:28.559
That is the end of Part 1.

00:06:28.559 --> 00:06:32.000
You now have half a minute to check your answers to Part 1.

